# THE EVA CONSOLE - what a console BECOMES when what it boarded has no floor.
#
# The sibling of `boarding_crew_console.mast`, and the same job: `eva_go_out` changes what
# the console IS - CONSOLE_TYPE, the crew seat, and the ship it is assigned to, which is
# now the SUIT - but it cannot send the GUI task anywhere, because a reroute needs a LABEL
# and labels are MAST.
#
# Grid boarding watches `ship_internal_view`, a deck seen from above. A relic has no deck,
# so this watches `3dview`: the suit, in the ruin, from outside it. Everything right of the
# view is the same xESS, and the Nav app on it is the only flight control there is.


# THE WAY BACK FROM THE ePADD, installed once, at the top level - the same substitution
# `boarding_crew_console.mast` installs for the grid console, for the same reason: this
# console is reached by a reroute rather than a tab, so without it Back has no name to
# land on and the ePADD declares no back tab at all.
gui_tab_back_while_boarded("eva_crew")


# One console, one crew member, one suit.
#
# `gui_eva_console()` is the library's builder: the 3D view in its own section - an engine
# widget draws at its own size over anything sharing its row - and beside it who you are,
# which chamber you are in, and where you can go.
=== eva_crew_console
    gui_eva_console()
    # A FUNCTION, not a jump back to this label. Repainting re-sends every widget on the
    # screen to this console over the network, and a watcher would do it forever.
    on change eva_console_revision():
        gui_eva_console_tick()
    await gui()
    ->END


# Suiting up opens the view. `//shared/signal` because the signal is emitted once per
# console that goes out, and `gui_reroute_client` takes that one client - running this
# per-console as well would reroute everybody every time anybody suited up.
//shared/signal/eva_went_out
    ->END if EVA_SUIT is None or EVA_SUIT == 0
    gui_reroute_client(EVA_CLIENT, eva_crew_console)
    ->END


# And coming back takes it away again. `eva_go_in` has already restored CONSOLE_TYPE, put
# the client back on its own ship and deleted the suit; this is the screen half. Without
# it a returning console would sit here still, pointed at a ship that no longer exists.
//shared/signal/eva_came_back
    ->END if EVA_CONSOLE is None
    gui_reroute_client(EVA_CLIENT, eva_crew_home)
    ->END


# The way home, run ON the returning console so `client_id` is that console.
#
# The recipe is `boarding_crew_home`'s, not a new one, and it was paid for there:
# `common_console_show` drops a console with no player ship into the CLIENT console picker,
# whose `select_console` reads a task variable the server's GUI task never ran - a
# NameError and a dead screen. The main screen is usually client 0, which has no ship.
=== eva_crew_home
    # `ecc_` PREFIXED, and that is not style. `eva_home_ship` is a LIBRARY FUNCTION and so
    # a MAST global; assigning to one is `Variable assignment to a keyword`, a COMPILE
    # error that takes the whole story to 0 labels.
    ecc_back = get_inventory_value(client_id, "CONSOLE_TYPE", "helm")
    jump show_main_game_screen if ecc_back == "mainscreen"
    ecc_ship = sbs.get_ship_of_client(client_id)
    jump show_main_game_screen if ecc_ship == 0 or not has_role(ecc_ship, "__player__")
    jump common_console_show


# The EVA console as a TAB, so Back has a name to land on. At the END of the file, after
# every other label: labels fall through, so inserting one between two existing labels is
# never a safe append.
//gui/tab/eva_crew
    jump eva_crew_console
